  "what_it_found": "The rabbit model is the only model to date that has achieved laboratory to clinical translational success for tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) treatment.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "To date, this is the only model that was able to achieve laboratory to clinical translational success.",
      "topic": "Translational success"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Use of caffeinol as a cytoprotective agent in rat models yielded exciting results, which led to clinical trials.",
      "topic": "Rat model results"
    },
    {
      "quote": "However, caffeinol given with tPA in rabbits leads to increased hemorrhage.",
      "topic": "Rabbit hemorrhage risk"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Caffeinol alone does not prove to be neuroprotective, as vasodilation by itself is not efficacious.",
      "topic": "Caffeinol efficacy"
    },
    {
      "quote": "However, vasodilation combined with thrombolysis (caffeinol with tPA) poses an increased risk of hemorrhage.",
      "topic": "Combined risk"
    },
    {
      "quote": "For a more translational approach to study neuroprotection and neuroprotective agents in human trials, it is necessary to demonstrate the efficacy of the procedure and purported agents in several animal models.",
